[Theorem p1q of arXiv:2509.24452, 'A family of non-uniform distributions on the set of parking functions generated by random '] Consider $\pi_1$ under the measure $\mathcal{P}_n^{(q)}$, with $q\in(0,1)$.
Then
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{P}_n^{(q)}(\pi_1=k)=(1-q)q^{k-1},\ k\in\mathbb{N}.
\end{equation}
That is, $\pi_1$ converges in distribution to the geometric distribution with parameter $1-q$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

