{"artifact_digest":"sha256:8539e354825369e3cded32d635dd3e232d320c25ce99b13a5dc838b2a2f9d032","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/85/8539e354825369e3cded32d635dd3e232d320c25ce99b13a5dc838b2a2f9d032.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:59df7a4b42ef028b7a3a4a2e444d62f34ca264dd0554d891326d4c23d0b3affa","bundle_entry_key":"2607-15125-theorem-7-plane-inversion","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_071","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_071.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-15125-proposition-7-plane-inversion","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Since $k_{a_+}\left( \frac1z\right) = -\frac{a_-}{a_+}k_{a_-}(z)$, it is possible to take $a\in(-1/2,0)$ without loss of generality.

By the chain rule:
    \begin{align}
        (k^{-1})'(k(z)) &= \frac{1}{k'(z)} = -a z^{1+\frac1a}\sqrt{z^2+1}
         \intertext{so, $k^{-1}$ must satisfy the differential equation}
         (k^{-1})'(z)    &= -a(k^{-1}(z))^{1+\frac1a}\sqrt{(k^{-1}(z))^2 + 1} \eqqcolon g(k^{-1}(z)), 
         \intertext{with}
         g(u) &= -a u^{1+\frac1a}\sqrt{ u^2 + 1 } 
    \end{align}
