[Corollary 29 (Equivalent modified formulations) of arXiv:2607.26377, '\textbf{Entire Logarithmic Signatures\\ of Bounded-Variation Paths in Finite Dimensions}'] Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real normed space, let $\gamma:[0,T]\to V$ be a continuous tree-reduced bounded-variation path, and put $v=\gamma_T-\gamma_0$. If $v\neq0$, then the following formulations are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
\item centred weak path conjugacy to a line in the sense of \cref{def:two-conjugacies};
\item conjugacy to $\e^v$ in the reduced bounded-variation path group;
\item a signature identity $S(\gamma)=a\e^v a^{-1}$ with $a\in\Sl(V)$.
\end{enumerate}
When $v=0$, the corresponding formulations all reduce to the constant reduced path. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2605.09459, 'Multipoint connectivity in the branching interlacement process'] For $d \geq 9$, and $k \geq 3$. Let
  \begin{equation}

    n(k,d) = \left\lceil\frac{d(k-1)}{4}\right\rceil - (k-2).
  \end{equation}
  Assume that the reproduction law is a mean $1$ distribution with finite $2\lceil d(k-1)/4\rceil$  moment, and that the jump law is centered, has finite range and its support does not generate any strict subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}^d$. Then almost surely, for every $k$ points of the interlacement, there exist $n(k,d)$ trajectories of the interlacement that connect them. Futhermore, almost surely, there exist $k$ points of the interlacement that are not connected using less than $n(k,d)$ trajectories. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop 13 (Product measure factorial moments) of arXiv:2506.12632, 'Point process convergence of extremes in $K$-symmetric exclusion'] Let $A \in \sB_\R$ and $n \in \N$. If $\eta, \chi \in \sX_K$ such that $\eta(x) \le \chi(x)$ for all $x \in \Z$, then  
\begin{equation}
	0 \le (\mu_t^\eta(A))^n - \E_\eta[N_t^{(n)}(A^n)] \le (\mu_t^{\chi}(A))^n - \E_{\chi}[N_t^{(n)}(A^n)]. 
\end{equation}
Moreover, if $\nu \in \sP_K$ and we let $\chi_\nu = K1_{\{\nu > 0\}}$, then  
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
	&-K^2 {n \choose 2} (\mu_{t/K}^{\chi_\nu}(A))^{n-2} \tau_{t}(\{\nu > 0\}, A) \\
	&\le ( \mu_{t/K}^\nu(A) )^n - \E_{\nu}[N_{t/K}^{(n)}(A^n)] \le (\mu_{t/K}^{\chi_\nu}(A))^n - \E_{\chi_\nu}[N_{t/K}^{(n)}(A^n)]. 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

