[Corollary An-strict of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Assume the hypotheses of \textit{Claim~(An-monotone of the paper)}.
Fix $n\geq 0$ and define, on $[0,\tfrac{1}{2}]$, 
\begin{equation}
p_{n}(x)=(1-2x)(L_{n}(1-x)-L_{n}(x)),  
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
q_{n}(x)=|D_{n}(x)|=|L_{n}(x)+L_{n}(1-x)-1|.  
\end{equation}
If

\begin{enumerate}
\item $A_{n}>0$,

\item $p_{n}$ is strictly decreasing on $[0,\tfrac{1}{2}]$,

\item $q_{n}$ is strictly increasing on $[0,\tfrac{1}{2}]$,
\end{enumerate}

then 
\begin{equation}
\left( E_{n}-\frac{1}{2}\right) \left( E_{n}+E_{n+1}-1\right) >0,

\end{equation}
and consequently 
\begin{equation}
A_{n+1}<A_{n}.  
\end{equation}
In particular, if the above three conditions hold for every index $n$ with $
A_{n}>0$, then $\{A_{n}\}_{n\geq 0}$ is strictly decreasing until it reaches 
$0$.

