[Thm 4 of arXiv:2608.26723, 'Extrinsic upper bounds for the harmonic mean of eigenvalues in curved manifolds'] Let $M$ be an $m(\ge 2)$-dimensional closed orientable submanifold in an $n$-dimensional simply-connected Riemannian manifold $N$ of the sectional curvature $K_N \le \delta\le 0$.

	Then we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathfrak{H}(\lambda_1,\cdots,\lambda_m)\le \frac{m}{\vol(M)}\int_M\big(\delta+|\mathbf{H}|^2\big).
	\end{equation*}
	Equality holds implies that $\lambda_1=\cdots=\lambda_m$ and $M$ is minimally immersed in a geodesic sphere of $N$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop 6 of arXiv:2605.02420, '\textbf{Large time behavior of critical marked Hawkes processes with heavy tailed marks an'] a) For any $m\in\N$ and $\theta_1,\ldots, \theta_m, t_1,\ldots, t_m\in (0,\infty)$ we have
\begin{equation}
 E\exp\left\{-\sum_{k=1}^m\theta_k Y_{T}(t_k)\right\}=\exp\left\{-\mu\int_0^\infty g_T(t)dt\right\},

\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
  g_{T}(t):=1-Ee^{-\<N^t, f_T\>},

 \end{equation}
 with $f_T$ given by \eqref{e:f_sum} and \eqref{e:fT} and recalling that $N^t$ denotes the empirical measure of a branching particle system starting from a single particle at point $t$ (with no immigration). Moreover, the function $g_T$ satisfies
\begin{align}
  g_{ T}(t)=&1-e^{-f_T(t)}
G\left(1-\int_0^\infty g_{T}(t+s)\varphi(s)ds\right)
 \\
 =& 1-e^{-f_T(t)}+e^{-f_T(t)}\int_0^\infty g_T(t+s)\varphi (s)ds -e^{-f_T(t)}H \left(\int_0^\infty g_T(t+s)\varphi(s) ds\right)
 .
\end{align}
b) Let the function $h_T$ be given by
\begin{equation}h_T(t):=E\<N^t,f_T\>

\end{equation}
Then $h_T$ 
satisfies the equation
\begin{equation}

 h_{T} (t) =  f_T(t)+\int_0^\infty  h_{T}(t+s)\varphi (s)ds.
\end{equation}
Moreover, $h_T$ may be expressed explicitly as
\begin{equation}
 h_T(t)=f_T(t)+\int_0^\infty f_T(t+s) R(s)ds,

\end{equation}
where $R$ is the resolvent defined in \eqref{e:R}.\\
c) The functions $g_T$ and $h_T$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
 0\le g_T(t)\le h_T(t)\qquad t\ge 0.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Problem 16 (Townes certificate target) of arXiv:2607.04467, 'Bernstein Functions at Work:\\ Coalescents, Copulas, and Subordination'] For \(\eta=10^{-4}\), set
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
L(s)=\exp\!\Bigl(&-10\eta s+10^{-4}(e^{-10\eta s}-1)\\
&\quad +(e^{\eta s}-1)\Bigr),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
the bilateral Laplace transform of \(X=\eta(10+10P-Q)\), where \(P\) and \(Q\)
are independent and
\begin{equation}
P\sim\Pois(10^{-4}),\qquad Q\sim\Pois(1).
\end{equation}
Supply a complete certificate that \(L\) is completely monotone on \([0,1]\),
while
\(L^{1/100}\) is not completely monotone at \(s=0\). This would produce a real
ID mixing law whose mixed Poisson law is not \(100\)-divisible. (Stated as a problem by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

Let N take values in {0, 1, ..., m}, 1 <= k <= m, Y = binom(N, k), M_k = E[Y] and Q_k = E[Y^2]. If M_k > 0 then P(N >= k) = P(Y > 0) >= M_k^2/Q_k. Equality holds if and only if Y is constant on {Y > 0}, that is, N takes a single value s in {k, ..., m} on {N >= k}. M_k = 0 exactly when P(N >= k) = 0. For k = 1 this is the usual second-moment bound for P(N > 0); for k = m, Y = 1{N = m} and the bound is exact. Integer values matter: for X = 3/2 surely and k = 2, binom(X, 2) = X(X - 1)/2 = 3/8 is a positive constant, so the ratio is 1, while P(X >= 2) = 0.
