[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2609.01166, 'Nonparametric inference for density-dependent McKean--Vlasov diffusions'] Under Assumptions~\ref{ass:potential} and \ref{ass:coefficient}, there exists a unique \(C^1\) zero-flux stationary density for $\xi$ given by
\begin{equation}
 \pi_\xi(x)=g_\xi^{-1}(\mu_\xi-\V(x)),
\end{equation}
where $\mu_\xi$ is the unique constant satisfying \( \int_{v_\star}^{\infty}g_\xi^{-1}(\mu_\xi-s)m_\V(s)\,\dd s=1 \). Equivalently, $q_\xi(s):=g_\xi^{-1}(\mu_\xi-s)$ solves
\begin{equation}
 q_\xi'(s)=-q_\xi(s)\xi(q_\xi(s)) \quad \text{for} \quad s>v_\star,
\end{equation}
where $a_\xi:=q_\xi(v_\star)$ is chosen to satisfy 
\begin{equation}
\int_{v_\star}^{\infty}q_\xi(s)m_\V(s)\,\dd s=1.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:no-hit-sde of arXiv:2607.00800, 'Brownian motion in Minkowski normed spaces'] Let $s> 0$ and $X$ be a weak solution of (equation SDE-linear of the paper) with initial datum $f_Q^z(s,x)\,\mathrm d x$, defined on a stochastic basis $(\Omega, \mathcal F, (\mathcal F_t)_{t\geq 0}, \mathbb{P})$.
Then, for all $T>0$, we have
\begin{equation}
\mathbb P\bigl(X_t\ne z\text{ for all }t\in[0,T]\bigr)=1.
\end{equation}

[Lemma lem:word-cost of arXiv:2608.09368, 'A proof of Ross's conjecture for two-site moving-target search'] The expected search cost of following the sequence $w$ until either the
target is detected or all letters of $w$ have been used is
\begin{equation}
 J_w(p)=\rho(p)R_wc.

\end{equation}
In particular, $J_w$ is affine in $p$.

[Proposition 13 (Convergence of the drift term) of arXiv:2607.01629, 'Quadratic fluctuations of speed-change Kawasaki dynamics'] For every function $f\in C^\infty\left(\mathbb{T}^{2d}\right)$, the sequence of $\{\mathcal A^N(f)\}_{N \in \N}$ admits a limit $\mathcal A(f)$ as $N\to\infty$ in $D\left([0,T],\R\right)$. Every limit $\mathcal A(f)$ is in $C\left([0,T],\R\right)$ and satisfies that 
		\begin{equation*}
			\mathcal A_t(f)=\int_0^t \mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathbf D(\rho)Q_s(\partial^2 _{1,2}f)\right)\d s,
		\end{equation*}
        where $Q$ is the associated limit of $\{Q^{N}\}_{N \in \N}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

