[Theorem thm:first of arXiv:2605.05904, 'Schr\"odinger's problem with constraints'] Under Assumptions~(a:main of the paper) and (ass:f of the paper), the SDE 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
X_t &=x_0+\int_0^t \mathbf{I}(u, X_u)\cdot
\left\{\mathbf{b}(u,X_u) + \Sigma^2(u,X_u)\frac{(\nabla h(u,X_u))^*}{h(u,X_u)}\right\} du\\
&\hspace{1cm}+\int_0^t \mathbf{I}(u, X_u)\cdot\Sigma(u,X_u)d\beta_u
\end{split}
	\end{equation}
 admits a unique weak solution,	where $A^*$ stands for the transpose of matrix $A$, $x_0=(z_0,z_0)$, and $\beta$ is a 2-dimensional standard Brownian motion. The corresponding   law of the solution coincides with the unique minimiser $P^*$ of the \sch problem $\mathcal{S}_E(\mu_0,\mu_1)$ in (equation P:unconstrainedeps of the paper). In particular, for every $t\in [0,1]$, $\frac{dP^*}{dR}|_{\cF_t}=h(t,X_t)$.

[Lemma lem:ineq-pivot-site-bond of arXiv:2607.14993, 'Sharp phase transition for percolation with short-range dependencies'] Let $0 < p, q < 1$ and $n \in \mathbb N^*$. Then for $e \in V_1$ with neighbours $x, y$,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb P(e \text{ pivotal for } A_n) \leqslant \frac qp (\mathbb P(x \text{ pivotal for } A_n) + \mathbb P(y \text{ pivotal for } A_n))
  \end{equation}
  and, for $x \in V_0$,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb P(x \text{ pivotal for } A_n) \leqslant \frac {(2d-1)p} {q (1-p)^{2d-1}} \sum\limits_{e \in \mathcal N(x)} \mathbb P(e \text{ pivotal for } A_n),
  \end{equation}
  where $\mathcal N(x)$ denotes the set of vertices adjacent to vertex~$x$\footnote{Note that $\mathcal N(x) \subset V_1$ corresponds to the set of edges adjacent to $x$ in the original graph.}.

[Definition Lower_env of arXiv:2511.15893, 'On a Class of Dynamical Poisson-Voronoi Tessellations'] Associated with the marked Poisson point process of the stations
$\tilde{\Phi}=\sum_{i\in \mathbb{N}}\delta_{(R_i,\alpha_i)}$, define the function

$$t\mapsto L(t):=\inf_{i\in \mathbb{N}} f((R_i,\alpha_i), t).$$

The stochastic process $\{{\mathcal L }(t)=(t,L(t))\}_{t\in \mathbb{R}}$ is called the lower envelope process, where the random variable $L(t)$ represents the distance of the closest station at time $t$. 
The \textbf{lower envelope} is the random closed set on $\mathbb{H}^+$ defined as 

\begin{equation}
	{\mathcal L }_e:=\{(t, L(t)),\ t\in \mathbb R\} \subset \mathbb{H}^+.{\nonumber}

\end{equation}

