body:
Let X = A union B where A and B are both CLOSED in X (or both open, or more generally a locally finite closed cover). Let f : A -> Y and g : B -> Y be continuous and agree on A intersect B. Then the combined function h : X -> Y defined by h = f on A and h = g on B is well defined and continuous. The closedness (or openness, or local finiteness) of the pieces is essential: for an arbitrary cover by two sets the conclusion is false, as the cover of R by the rationals and the irrationals shows, where two constant functions paste to a nowhere continuous function.
The pieces must be finitely many closed sets, or arbitrarily many open sets, or a locally finite closed family;
and the functions must AGREE on the overlap, which is what makes h well defined.

[Proposition p:cond of arXiv:2605.20587, 'Persistence and entropic repulsion of stationary Gaussian fields with spectral singularity'] Let $\alpha \in [0,d)$, and let $f$ be an SGF on $\mathbb{R}^d$ or $\mathbb{Z}^d$ satisfying one of the conditions~(item:1 of the paper), (item:2 of the paper), (item:3 of the paper) and (item:4 of the paper) in Theorem~(t: univ pro of the paper). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\delta \mapsto \delta^{-\alpha} \mu[B(\delta)]$ is slowly varying as $\delta \to 0$; and
    \item $T \mapsto T^{-\alpha} \text{\textup{Cap}}_f(B(T))$ is slowly varying as $T \to \infty$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular (equation e:blowup of the paper), (equation e:capcon of the paper) and (equation e:doub of the paper) hold. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary 16 of arXiv:2608.30304, '2608.30304'] Let $a,b\in[-\tfrac12,A_0]$ and suppose
\begin{equation}
1+x>\frac2{(n+1)^2}\qquad\text{and}\qquad 1-x>\frac2{(n+1)^2}.
\end{equation}
If $n\ge1$ then
\begin{equation}
F_{n,a,b}(x)\le\theta_L\bigl\{F_{n-1,a,b+1}(x)+F_{n-1,a,b+2}(x)\bigr\},
\qquad \theta_L=5.6 ,
\end{equation}
with $\theta_L=3.3$ if $n\ge2$; and if $n\ge2$ then
\begin{equation}
F_{n,a,b}(x)\le\theta_2\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^2F_{n-2,a+i,b+j}(x),
\qquad \theta_2=17 .
\end{equation}

[Proposition 16 (Weak geodesic convexity) of arXiv:2604.22159, 'Adapted optimal transport between filtered Gaussian processes'] Let $\bX_0 = \bG^{a_0, L_0}, \bX_1 = \bG^{a_1, L_1} \in \cFG$, where $(a_0, L_0), (a_1, L_1) \in \bR^{Nd} \times \mathscr{L}(N, d)$. Given $Q \in \mathscr{Q}(L_0, L_1)$, define $\hat{L}_1 = L_1 Q \in \mathscr{L}(N, d)$. For $u \in [0, 1]$, let $\hat{\bX}_u = \bG^{\hat{a}_u, \hat{L}_u} \in \cFG$, where
\begin{equation} 
\hat{a}_u = (1 - u) a_0 + u a_1 \quad \text{and} \quad \hat{L}_u = L_0 + u (\hat{L}_1 - L_0).
\end{equation}
Then $\AW_2(\bX_0, \hat{\bX}_0) = \AW_2(\bX_1, \hat{\bX}_1) = 0$ and
\begin{equation} 
\AW_2(\hat{\bX}_u, \hat{\bX}_v) = |u - v| \AW_2(\hat{\bX}_0, \hat{\bX}_1), \quad u, v \in [0, 1].
\end{equation}
In particular, $([\hat{\bX}_u])_{u \in [0, 1]}$ is a constant speed geodesic from $[\bX_0]$ to $[\bX_1]$ contained in $\FG$.

