[Theorem 5 (\cite{Kac,Pleijel}) of arXiv:2507.06186, '2507.06186'] If $\partial D$ is sufficiently regular (e.g., smooth), then
\begin{align}

\msf T_0(t)=\tfrac{\msf{A}(D)}{2\pi}t^{-1}
-\tfrac{\msf{L}(\partial D)}{4\sqrt{2\pi}}t^{-1/2}+O(1)\qquad\text{as }t\to0,
\end{align}
where $\msf L(\partial D)$ is the length of the curve
$\partial D$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:square-constrained-bonds of arXiv:2608.23545, 'Eulerian walkers on $\Z^2$ have range exponent $2/3$'] There are constants $c,C>0$ such that the following holds for every $x\in\mathbb Z^2$
		and every integer $r\geq1$.  For bond percolation on $\mathbb Z^2$ with parameter
		$1/2$,
		\begin{equation}
			\mathbb P_{1/2}\left\{
			\begin{array}{c}
				\text{there is an open path from $x$ to $\{y:|y-x|_\infty=r\}$ inside}\\
				\text{$\{y:|y-x|_\infty\leq r\}$ containing no translate of $P_\star$,}\\
				\text{in either direction, as a consecutive subpath}
			\end{array}
			\right\}
			\leq Ce^{-cr}\, .
		\end{equation}

