{"artifact_digest":"sha256:fe955f52b72c6bf5a0c3cc0120bb2d21dfe12f954e0f6d3d22c0840f7efdd6be","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/fe/fe955f52b72c6bf5a0c3cc0120bb2d21dfe12f954e0f6d3d22c0840f7efdd6be.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:5fe61c4a662b4c1757fa2113406412a70097b4367196008a241511a820a6f715","bundle_entry_key":"2603-05491-theorem-40-no-bad-steps","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_101","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_101.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2603-05491-proposition-40-no-bad-steps","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Observe that, due to the invariance under translation along the boundary, the probability of the event considered is independent of the peeling algorithm $\mathcal{A}$. We proceed by induction on $k \ge 1$. The base case $k = 1$ follows directly from Proposition~(no_event_H_or_O of the paper) and Proposition~(Finite_holes_filled_by_finite_maps of the paper).

