{"artifact_digest":"sha256:fd18d9bc96c26a8bf98f2cbcdedf1c7a904de8fb5fb006b19197bf2d96881a3b","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/fd/fd18d9bc96c26a8bf98f2cbcdedf1c7a904de8fb5fb006b19197bf2d96881a3b.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:2654dfd9de99af3f5e50ed6b06af6f79943e5c5617681ee008f9b0c3c259eed4","bundle_entry_key":"2608-26803-lemma-21-banach-to-hausdorff","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_113","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_113.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-26803-lemma-21-banach-to-hausdorff","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Pick a countable closed cover $\{ B_l \}$ of $B$ such that for all $l$ the map $F|_{B_l}$ is proper. This can be arranged since by an elementary application of the inverse function theorem, $F$ can be locally put in a form $F(x, k) = (x, f(x, k))$, where $k$ is in a finite-dimensional vector space; the properness follows by restricting $k$ to a compact subset. We will show that $A_l \coloneq j^{-1}(F(B_l))$ is nowhere dense. 

  Since $F|_{B_l}$ is proper, $F(B_l)$ is closed and so is $A_l$. It therefore remains to show that $A_l$ has empty interior. Suppose that $v_0 \in A_l$ and let $U$ be its open neighborhood. Set
