{"artifact_digest":"sha256:9a6a2697a8773fdd4b7e9c3b88c571c8fcabbef8bf09fed33f1d81cf63e911c7","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/9a/9a6a2697a8773fdd4b7e9c3b88c571c8fcabbef8bf09fed33f1d81cf63e911c7.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:96f680fcc456c42cb1d3b4d9af83f996ee647c549316d908521c45ebf361d140","bundle_entry_key":"2609-01346-lemma-4-unique-crossing","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_141","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_141.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-01346-lemma-4-unique-crossing","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: \medskip\noindent\textbf{Step 1: construct the regular $u$-equation.}
Set
\[
 \varepsilon=\sin\theta_0\in\{-1,1\},
 \qquad \phi=\theta-\theta_0.
\]
Then
\[
 \cos(\theta_0+\phi)=-\varepsilon\sin\phi,
 \qquad
 \sin(\theta_0+\phi)=\varepsilon\cos\phi,
\]
and \eqref{eq:profile-system} becomes
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 \frac{dx}{ds}&=-\varepsilon\sin\phi,\qquad
 \frac{dr}{ds}=\varepsilon\cos\phi,\\
 \frac{d\phi}{ds}&=\frac{\varepsilon r^{k-1}}{A\sin^{k-1}\phi}
 \left(x\cos\phi+r\sin\phi-B\frac{\sin^k\phi}{r^k}\right).
 \end{aligned}
