[Lemma 41 (Frostman's Lemma) of arXiv:2512.07202, 'Hitting Probabilities for Hypoelliptic Differential Equations Driven by Fractional Brownia'] Let \( U \) be a compact metric space and fix \( 0 \leqslant \alpha < \infty \), \( 0 < \delta \leqslant \infty \). Then there exists a Radon measure \( \mu \) on \( U \) such that \( \mu(U) = \lambda^\delta_\alpha(U) \) and
\begin{equation}
\mu(E) \leqslant d(E)^\alpha \quad \text{for all } E \subseteq U \text{ with } d(E) < \delta.
\end{equation}
In particular, if \( \mathcal{H}_\alpha(U) > 0 \), then there exist \( \delta > 0 \) and a Radon measure \( \mu \) satisfying \eqref{cononmu} with \( \mu(U) > 0 \). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

