[Lemma lem:1530 of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] Consider the triples $(x_i,y_i,D_i)$ with $x_i\in D_i$ and $y_i\in \partial D_i$ that belong to one of the following three cases
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
        \item $x_i\in\partial\mathcal{B}_{-1}$ and $D_i=\mathcal B_l^\circ\setminus \mathcal B_{-4}$ with $l=2,3,4$.
        \item $x_i\in\partial\mathcal{B}_{-1}$ and $D_i=\mathcal B_2^\circ$.
        \item $x_i\in\partial\mathcal{B}_{2}$ and $D_i=\mathcal B_l^\circ\setminus \mathcal B_{-1}$ with $l=5,6,7$.
    \end{enumerate}
   Let $\eta_i$ be sampled according to $\mathbb{P}_{x_i\to y_i}^{D_i}$ and $\overline\eta:=\cup_{i=1}^k \eta_i$. Then, for each $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $0<C_1=C_1(\varepsilon,k)<\infty$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        \prod_{i=1}^k\mathbb P_{x_i\to y_i}^{D_i}\Big(K(p_{1/2,1}^{\overline\eta\cup\mathcal B_0})<C_1\Big)>1-\varepsilon.
    \end{equation}

[Theorem e: K gamma conversion of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $p\in[-d, \infty]$ and $\varepsilon>0$. Assume that $K$ and $\Phi$ fulfill the general assumptions in this subsection and the assumptions made in Theorem~(t:main theorem of the paper). In particular, suppose that $K$ is a probability kernel and that $\Phi$ is (beyond) $p$-uniform. Then $K^{(\gamma)}\Phi$ is (beyond) $p$-uniform for every $\gamma\in\R$. In the case $p=\infty$,  $K^{(\gamma)}\Phi$ is $\infty$-uniform with radius $\varepsilon$ for every $\gamma\in\R$ such that Condition~(c:condition square-integrable stealthy of the paper) is fulfilled with respect to $\tilde\varepsilon:=|\gamma|\varepsilon$, in particular, for any $\gamma\in[-1,1]$.
    \begin{proof}
        All assertions are basically a consequence of the fact that
        \begin{equation}
            \int g(x)\, K^{(\gamma)\ast}(y, dx) = \int g(\gamma x)\, K^\ast(y, dx) 
        \end{equation}
        for $\gamma\in\R, y\in\R^d$ and a suitable function $g:\R^d\to\C$. It implies that Condition~(c:condition square-integrable general with p of the paper) or~(c:condition square-integrable Markov with p of the paper) holds with respect to $K^{(\gamma)}$ if it does with respect to $K$, where one also has to apply Lemma~(l:stronlgy log-dominating modification of the paper) to deal with $\rho$. In Condition~(c:condition square-integrable stealthy of the paper), there is only a slight change of the exponent. It is also implied that $\Psi_q$ only differs by a factor of $\gamma^q$ if one defines it with respect to $K^{(\gamma)}$ instead of $K$, which does not affect $(p-2q)$-uniformity or the radius if $p=\infty$. In combination, this allows for an application of Theorem~(t:main theorem of the paper) with $K$ replaced by $K^{(\gamma)}$ to conclude the proof.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

