[Corollary cor:consistency-var of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $ \mathbf{X} _n$ be adapted to $\mathcal{F}$ with constant conditional mean $ \mathbb E [ \mathbf{X} _n|\mathcal{F}_{n-1}] =  \mathbf{M} $ and conditional variance $ \mathbb E [( \mathbf{X} _n -  \mathbf{M} )^2|\mathcal{F}_{n-1}] =  \mathbf{V} $. Further, assume that
    \begin{equation}
        \sum_{n=1}^\infty n^{-1-\delta}  \mathbb E [ \| \mathbf{X} _n \|^{2+2\delta} | \mathcal{F}_{n-1}  ] < \infty
    \end{equation}
    almost surely for some $\delta \in (0,1]$.
    Then, the sample variance
    \begin{equation}
        \overline  \mathbf{V} _n = \frac 1 n \sum_{i=1}^n ( \mathbf{X} _i - \overline{ \mathbf{X} }_n)^2
    \end{equation}
    converges to $ \mathbf{V} $ almost surely.

