body:
[Theorem thm:modelcriterion of arXiv:2608.26564, 'Real ideal points, Conway spheres, and left-orderable Dehn fillings'] Under the above hypotheses, the four-longitude decomposition is in positive parabolic normal form along one, and hence both, real half-branches if and only if $c_1c_2\alpha_1\alpha_2 < 0$. Moreover, for each real half-branch $B$, there is a sign $\sigma_B \in \{\pm 1\}$ such that its limiting height vector is 
\begin{equation*}
	(h_1, h_2, h_3, h_4) = \sigma_B(\eta_1, \mathrm{sgn}(c_1c_2)\eta_2, \eta_3, \mathrm{sgn}(c_1c_2)\eta_4)
\end{equation*}
Consequently, height-cancellation holds along one, and hence both, real half-branches if and only if $\eta_1+\eta_3 + \mathrm{sgn}(c_1c_2)(\eta_2 + \eta_4) = 0$.

