[Corollary cor:heightone-singlecross of arXiv:2608.09368, 'A proof of Ross's conjecture for two-site moving-target search'] Let $H,L$ be a height-one pair and let
\begin{equation}
 d:=(R_H-R_L)c=\binom{d_1}{d_2}.

\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 d_2<0<d_1

\end{equation}
is impossible.  Equivalently, the affine function
\begin{equation}
 J_H(p)-J_L(p)=pd_1+(1-p)d_2

\end{equation}
cannot cross zero from negative to positive as $p$ increases.

