{"artifact_digest":"sha256:7bf6614aa9763f6be497ffb409b29fb61bc073bfb97249e5d9f9d92e52ff0d63","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/7b/7bf6614aa9763f6be497ffb409b29fb61bc073bfb97249e5d9f9d92e52ff0d63.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:c237ede52743929c4d98e417e4d923955d77e84f16778f895e1087edff83b191","bundle_entry_key":"2604-22159-theorem-45-adapted-gelbrich-bound-under-t","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_018","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_018.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2604-22159-theorem-45-adapted-gelbrich-bound-under-t","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We assume without loss of generality that $\mu$ and $\nu$ have mean zero. Let $L, M \in \mathscr{L}_{+}(Nd)$ be respectively the minimal Cholesky factors of the covariance matrices $A$ and $B$ of $\mu$ and $\nu$. Let $\pi \in \Cpl_{\mathrm{bc}}(\mu, \nu)$ and $(X, Y) \sim \pi$. Define $\epsilon^X = L^{\ominus} X$ and $\epsilon^Y = M^{\ominus} Y$. By Lemma \ref{lem:proj_cov}, for each $t \in [N]$ we have

\begin{equation} 
 \Cov(\epsilon_t^X) \preceq I \quad \text{and} \quad  \Cov(\epsilon_t^Y) \preceq I.  
\end{equation}

Consider the transport cost
\begin{equation} 
