body:
[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2606.11389, 'Instability of a nonlinear oscillator with small friction and small additive noise'] Suppose $g: \R^2 \setminus \{(0,0)\} \to [0,\infty)$ satisfies  $\int_{r > \delta} g(x,y)d\mu(x,y) < \infty$ for each fixed $\delta >0$ and $g(x,y) = {\cal O}(r^k)$ as $r \to 0$.  Then   
  \begin{equation} 
    \int_{\R^2} \chi_\e(h) g(x,y) d\mu(x,y) \begin{cases}
          \to \int_{\R^2}g(x,y) d\mu(x,y)  & \mbox{ if } k > -2, \\
          = {\cal O}(\log 1/\e) & \mbox{ if } k = -2, \\
          = {\cal O}(\e^{(k+2)/2}) & \mbox{ if } k <-2
          \end{cases}
  \end{equation}  and
   \begin{equation} 
    \int_{\e \le h \le 2\e} g(x,y) d\mu(x,y)  = \begin{cases} {\cal O}(\e^{(k+2)/2}) & \mbox{ if } k \neq -2, \\
                                                              {\cal O}(\log 1/\e)  & \mbox{ if } k = -2                     
                                        \end{cases}
                                         \end{equation}    
as $\e \to 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

