{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1193913bf03d16c9379fa65e75843dc683c64fe83ee9c81ea06d4629a5f9205a","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/11/1193913bf03d16c9379fa65e75843dc683c64fe83ee9c81ea06d4629a5f9205a.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:b0ee2168a5715a9be75b88c0daa1a61421d80b691954e4f71a16dbcebb076a5e","bundle_entry_key":"2508-04954-lemma-14-equalfnbootstrap","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_021","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_021.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2508-04954-lem-14-equalfnbootstrap","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $T: \Omega\subset {\mathbb R}^2 \to {\mathbb R}^2$ be the map given by $T(x,y) = (x,g(x,y))$, and let $V=T(\Omega)$. 
