body:
[Definition def_0_limdif of arXiv:2411.13972, 'The Stochastic Bessel operator at high temperatures'] ~\\ \emph{
The limiting diffusion $r_\mu$ is defined as follows:}
\begin{itemize}
	\item \emph{It starts at $0$ at time $0$: $r_\mu(0) = 0$.}
	\item \emph{It then follows a Brownian motion with drift $a/4$  reflected downwards at $0$ (built from $W$)
\begin{align}
	r^+:= W(t) + at/4 -  \sup_{s \leqslant t} \big(W(s) +as/4\big)\vee 0\,
\end{align}
until its first hitting time of the critical line $c_\mu(t)$ from (equation criticalline of the paper).}
\item \emph{If it reaches $t \mapsto c_\mu(t)$ in a finite time, it immediately restarts at $0$ at this time, then follows a reflected downwards at $0$ Brownian motion with another drift $-(a+1)/4$ (also built from $W$) 
\begin{align}
	r^-:= W(t) - (a+1)t/4 -  \sup_{s \leqslant t} \big(W(s) - (a+1)s/4\big)\vee 0\
\end{align}
until its first hitting time of the critical line $c_\mu(t)$ from (equation criticalline of the paper).}
\end{itemize}
\emph{And so on, $r_\mu$ alternating between $r^+$ and $r^-$ each time it hits $t \mapsto c_\mu(t)$ and restarts at $0$.
Note that the probability that $r^+$ reaches the critical line decreases with time. On the other hand, since $a>0$, $r^-$ almost surely hits the critical line in a finite time.
}

[Proposition 26 of arXiv:2511.13404, 'Asymptotic stability and mean ergodicity of Feller processes on Polish spaces'] Suppose that there exists some $z\in \X$ such that $\Phi$ is Ces\`aro eventually continuous at $z$ and for any $\varepsilon>0$, 
		\begin{equation}\tag{$\mathcal{C}_3$}
			\limsup\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty}Q_t(z,B(z,\varepsilon))>0.
		\end{equation}	
		Then $\{Q_t(z,\cdot)\}_{t\ge0}$ is tight, which implies that there exists an invariant measure for  $\Phi$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition def : Topology/GMT/Gradient of arXiv:2412.03320, 'Large deviation principle at speed $n$ for the random metric in first-passage percolation'] Let $D \in \RegPD[\mu]$ and $z\in X$. The \emph{gradient by paths} of $D$ at $z$ is defined as the function
	\begin{align}
		(\Pgrad D)_z : \mathbb{R}^d &\longrightarrow \intervallefo0\infty \eol
		u&\longmapsto \inf\set
			{\liminf_{t\to0} \frac{D\p{\restriction{\gamma}{\intervalleff0t} }}{t} }
			{\begin{array}{c} \gamma : \intervalleff0T \rightarrow X\text{ Lipschitz}, \gamma(0)=z, \\ \gamma \text{ is differentiable at }t=0\text{ and }\gamma'(0)=u \end{array} }
	\end{align}

[Definition def:l_and_bb_pin of arXiv:2606.11951, 'On Skorokhod Problems for Reflected and Singular Stochastic Heat Equations'] Let $x \in (0,1)$, and let $l^x \in C_0$ be defined by
	\begin{equation*}
		l^x_{\xi} \coloneqq \frac{\xi}{x} \, \mathds{1}_{[0,x]}(\xi) + \frac{1-\xi}{1-x} \, \mathds{1}_{(x,1]}(\xi), \quad \xi \in [0,1].
	\end{equation*}
	Consequently, we define the stochastic process $\beta^{\mathrm{pin},x} \coloneqq \big(\beta_{\xi} - \beta_x  l_{\xi}^x \big)_{\xi \in [0,1]}$. Note that the paths of $\beta^{\mathrm{pin},x}$ are in $C_0$, and we have $\beta^{\mathrm{pin},x}_x = 0$ by construction.

[Corollary Coroll-probtauUN-001b of arXiv:2412.08932, 'Gaussian heat kernel asymptotics for conditioned random walks'] Under the assumptions of Theorem (Theor-probtauUN-001 of the paper),
 for any $a\in \supp V$, it holds that, as $n\to\infty$, uniformly over $x\in [a,\infty)$,  
\begin{align} 
 \mathbb{P} \left( \tau_{x}>n\right) \sim \frac{V(x)L\left(  \frac{x}{\sigma \sqrt{n}} \right) }{\sigma \sqrt{n}}.
\end{align}
In particular, as $V(0)>0$, the equivalence (equation UniformCondtau-001a of the paper) holds true uniformly for $x\in [0,\infty)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Defi def: chi-square distance of arXiv:2504.02207, 'Finite-Time behaviors of M/M/$n$ Queue: Mixing Time, Mean Queue Length and Tail Bounds'] Given two distributions $P, Q$ whose supports are the subset of the countable set $\cS$ and $P$ is absolutely continuous w.r.t $Q$ 

, the Chi-square distance of $P$ with respect to $Q$ is given by
\begin{align}
    \chi^2 \br{P,Q} = \sum_{x \in \cS} \frac{(P(x)-Q(x))^2}{Q(x)}.
\end{align}

