[Lemma lm:v-is-concave of arXiv:2605.16162, 'Deconfinement For $\mathrm{SO}(3)$ Lattice Yang--Mills at Strong Coupling'] The function defined in equation (equation eq:log-Wilson-loop of the paper) satisfies the following properties.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $v(R,T)$ is concave in both variables. That is for all $R,T,r,t \in \N$ with $r \leq R$ and $t \leq T$,
        \begin{equs}
            v(R,T) &\geq \frac{1}{2}(v(R+r,T)+v(R-r,T)),\\
            v(R,T) & \geq \frac{1}{2}(v(R,T+t)+v(R,T-t)).
        \end{equs}
        \item The function $v(R,T)$ is subadditive as a function of $T$ and thus 
        \begin{equs}
            V(R):= \lim_{T \to \infty}\frac{1}{T}v(R,T)
        \end{equs}
        exists. In addition, $V(R)$ is concave.
        \item $v(R,T)$ is non-decreasing in both variables and as a consequence $V(R)$ is also non-decreasing.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

